BT /F2 22 Tf 72 710.0 Td (Chapter 1: Describing Motion) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.5 Tf 72 672.3 Td (Motion is everywhere around us, from a rolling ball to the planets circling the Sun. To) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.5 Tf 72 656.8 Td (study motion we must first measure where an object is and how quickly its position) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.5 Tf 72 641.3 Td (changes. Physicists describe motion using a small set of precise quantities.) Tj ET
BT /F2 15 Tf 72 611.7 Td (Speed and Velocity) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.5 Tf 72 583.5 Td (Speed is the distance an object travels in a given amount of time. Velocity is the speed) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.5 Tf 72 568.0 Td (of an object together with its direction of travel. Two cars can have the same speed but) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.5 Tf 72 552.4 Td (different velocity if they drive in opposite directions. This difference is important) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.5 Tf 72 536.9 Td (because direction changes the result of many calculations.) Tj ET
BT /F1 12 Tf 112 515.4 Td (v = d / t) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.5 Tf 72 491.2 Td (For example, a cyclist who covers 100 meters in 20 seconds has an average speed of 5) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.5 Tf 72 475.7 Td (meters per second. If the cyclist then turns around, the speed stays the same but the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.5 Tf 72 460.1 Td (velocity reverses.) Tj ET
BT /F2 15 Tf 72 430.6 Td (Acceleration) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.5 Tf 72 402.4 Td (Acceleration is the rate at which velocity changes over time. An object accelerates when) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.5 Tf 72 386.8 Td (it speeds up, slows down, or changes direction. Therefore a car moving around a bend at) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.5 Tf 72 371.3 Td (constant speed is still accelerating, because its direction keeps changing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 12 Tf 112 349.8 Td (a = \(v - u\) / t) Tj ET
endstream
